    "https://spartantalent.uncg.edu/postings/35749": {
      "title": "EUC Technical Services Coordinator",
      "description": "UNC Greensboro Employment Opportunities | EUC Technical Services Coordinator Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Current UNCG Employee Log In UNC System Careers EUC Technical Services Coordinator Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Position Number 013921 Functional Title EUC Technical Services Coordinator Position Type Staff Position Eclass EZ - EHRA Non - Exempt Permanent University Information Located in North Carolina’s third largest city, UNC Greensboro is among the most diverse, learner-centered public research universities in the state, with 18,000 students in eight colleges and schools pursuing more than 150 areas of undergraduate and over 200 areas of graduate study. UNCG continues to be recognized nationally for academic excellence, access, and affordability. UNCG is ranked No. 1 most affordable institution in North Carolina for net cost by the N.Y. Times and No. 1 in North Carolina for social mobility by The Wall Street Journal — helping first-generation and lower-income students find paths to prosperity. Designated an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University by the Association of Public and Land-grant Universities, UNCG is a community-engaged research institution with a portfolio of more than $67M in research and creative activity. The University’s 2,600 staff help create an annual economic impact for the Piedmont Triad region in excess of $1B. Primary Purpose of the Organizational Unit ITS Learning Technology and Client Services is committed to delivering responsive, high quality, customer-oriented services and support that enable a productive and stable instructional, research, and administrative environment for the students, faculty, and staff of UNCG . The unit supports the full lifecycle of technology solutions through the acquisition, implementation, management, and support of instructional, research, and business software applications, including enterprise learning, research, and office use technologies. It also maintains and supports technology-equipped learning spaces, open-access computer labs, and hyflex environments, while providing reliable internal and external ITS communication services, including web and mobile application development. In addition, the unit facilitates computer hardware and peripheral device acquisition, installation, and repair, and offers training, consultation, and direct client assistance to support effective use of technology across the university. Through active participation in campus-wide initiatives, the team ensures that evolving information technology needs are assessed and addressed in alignment with institutional priorities. The Elliott University Center, a department within the Division of Student Affairs at the University of North Carolina at Greensboro, serves as a central hub for campus life and a key contributor to the student experience. The Elliott University Center enhances student learning and development by providing spaces and opportunities for engagement, entertainment, social interaction, and personal growth. It supports an inclusive and diverse campus community through programs and services designed to foster connection and belonging. Additionally, the Elliott University Center provides a secure and accessible environment for meetings, conferences, workshops, and professional development, serving students, faculty, staff, and the broader Greensboro community. Position Summary The EUC Technical Services Coordinator reports to ITS and serves as a frontline technical resource providing audiovisual, presentation technology, and instructional technology support across Elliott University Center spaces and campus classrooms. This position ensures that technology systems in meeting rooms, conference spaces, and event venues are operational, well-maintained, and ready to support the diverse needs of the campus community. The position works with student organizations, university departments, faculty, and external clients to assess technology needs and resolve issues related to presentation equipment, hybrid meeting systems, and classroom technology. Responsibilities include supporting audiovisual systems, providing guidance to building users, and assisting with events and scheduled programs. This position collaborates with other members of the Classroom Technology team and the Technical Services team and campus partners to coordinate daily technology support and contribute to maintaining consistent, high-quality audiovisual and instructional technology services throughout the Elliott University Center. Minimum Qualifications Entry-level with Masters or Bachelors and 0-2 yrs experience or equivalent combination of education/experience Additional Required Certifications, Licensures, and Certificates Preferred Qualifications Graduation from a four-year college or university and two years’ experience working with audio-visual equipment and in the technology information field related to the position’s role. Special Instructions to Applicants Recruitment Range Salary commensurate with experience Org #-Department Info Technology Services - 23101 Job Open Date 08/20/2026 For Best Consideration Date Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes FTE 1.000 Type of Appointment Permanent If time-limited, please specify end date for appointment. Number of Months per Year 12 FLSA Non-Exempt Key Responsibilities ________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________ Percentage Of Time 30 Key Responsibility Audiovisual and Presentation Technology Support Essential Tasks Provide technical support for audiovisual, presentation, and hybrid meeting technology systems in Elliott University Center spaces including meeting rooms, conference spaces, the EUC Auditorium, and the Cone Ballroom. Troubleshoot and resolve technical issues with sound reinforcement systems, video displays, projection systems, microphones, and other AV equipment to ensure reliability for scheduled events and daily use. Assist users — including faculty, staff, student organizations, and external clients — with operating presentation technology, connecting personal devices, and launching meetings or presentations. Document recurring technical issues and solutions to improve service delivery and support consistent resolution of common problems. Percentage Of Time 25 Key Responsibility Event Technology Operations Essential Tasks Coordinate audiovisual and presentation technology support for events hosted within the Elliott University Center, including setup, operation, and breakdown of AV equipment. Meet event organizers to identify technical needs and help determine appropriate technology solutions for meetings, conferences, performances, and other programs. Support livestreaming and hybrid event technologies as required and assist with the technical logistics of large-scale events. Review the event schedule regularly to plan and prepare technology resources and ensure support is available for all scheduled events. Percentage Of Time 20 Key Responsibility Classroom and Meeting Room Technology Assistance Essential Tasks Respond to requests for in-person technical assistance in classrooms and EUC meeting spaces experiencing technology issues and provide timely resolution to minimize disruption. Provide walk-in and scheduled support for faculty, staff, and students needing assistance with room technology, presentation equipment, or hybrid meeting tools. Inspect and test classroom and meeting room technology systems on a regular basis to identify and address issues proactively before scheduled use. Communicate system issues, outages, and repair needs to the Technical Services team and assist with follow-up and resolution tracking. Percentage Of Time 15 Key Responsibility Technology Planning, Supervision and Professional Development Essential Tasks Maintain awareness of emerging trends and best practices in audiovisual systems, classroom technology, and event support to better serve the needs of campus programs. Participate in workshops, training opportunities, and professional development activities related to AV systems, event technology, and instructional technology. Assist in evaluating new technologies for potential implementation and make recommendations to improve existing systems and infrastructure. Support the development and documentation of policies, procedures, and user guides that promote consistent and effective use of technology within the EUC . Provide support to staff responsible for audiovisual services within the Elliott University Center. Percentage Of Time 10 Key Responsibility Collaboration and Customer Service Essential Tasks Work cooperatively with the Technical Services team, Reservations and Events staff, and other campus partners to coordinate technical support and ensure a positive experience for building users. Provide responsive, professional customer service to all EUC users, including faculty, staff, student organizations, and external clients. Participate in team meetings and communicate proactively regarding technology issues, event needs, and service improvements. Support the broader mission of the Elliott University Center as a hub for campus engagement by contributing to a welcoming, technology-ready environment. ADA Checklist ADA Checklist R for Rare (0-30%), O for Occasional (30-60%), F for Frequent (60-90%), C for Constant (90-100%). Physical Effort Hand Movement-Repetitive Motions - F, Reading - f, Writing - f, Hearing - f, Talking - f, Sitting - f, Bending - F, Squatting - F Work Environment Inside - c Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume/CV Cover Letter List of References Optional Documents Reference Letter 1 Reference Letter 2 Reference Letter 3 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Please indicate how you learned of the vacant position for which you are applying: UNCGjobsearch Website Greensboro News & Record Carolina Peacemaker Other Newspaper The Chronicle of Higher Education Inside Higher Ed Other Professional Journal NCWorks.gov UNC System Job Board Other Online Job Board Personal Networking Other * If you selected \"Other\", please provide the name of the resource here. (Open Ended Question) * Are you eligible to work in the United States without sponsorship? Yes No * Will you now or in the future require visa sponsorship for employment at the University of North Carolina in Greensboro? Yes No UNCG Human Resources UNC Greensboro 723 Kenilworth St. Greensboro, NC 27402-6170 Telephone 336.334.5009 Fax 336.334.5585 Back to top Copyright © 2025. UNC Greensboro. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://spartantalent.uncg.edu/postings/35284": {
      "title": "Admissions Counselor",
      "description": "UNC Greensboro Employment Opportunities | Admissions Counselor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Current UNCG Employee Log In UNC System Careers Admissions Counselor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Position Number 998191 Functional Title Admissions Counselor Position Type Staff Position Eclass EP - EHRA 12 mo leave earning University Information Located in North Carolina’s third largest city, UNC Greensboro is among the most diverse, learner-centered public research universities in the state, with 18,000 students in eight colleges and schools pursuing more than 150 areas of undergraduate and over 200 areas of graduate study. UNCG continues to be recognized nationally for academic excellence, access, and affordability. UNCG is ranked No. 1 most affordable institution in North Carolina for net cost by the N.Y. Times and No. 1 in North Carolina for social mobility by The Wall Street Journal — helping first-generation and lower-income students find paths to prosperity. Designated an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University by the Association of Public and Land-grant Universities, UNCG is a community-engaged research institution with a portfolio of more than $67M in research and creative activity. The University’s 2,600 staff help create an annual economic impact for the Piedmont Triad region in excess of $1B. Primary Purpose of the Organizational Unit The Office of Undergraduate Admissions is responsible for identifying, cultivating and recruiting potential applicants for admission as traditional first-time freshmen, transfers, and nontraditional adults; collecting and evaluating credentials; making decisions regarding individuals’ candidacies for admission to UNCG ; and positively affecting admitted students decisions to matriculate to UNCG . The Office of Undergraduate Admissions serves as a major public relations arm of the institution and helps to shape a positive image of the University through its interaction with parents, prospective students, high school and college counselors, alumni and other external agencies. The Office of Undergraduate Admissions communicates to the public the academic, programmatic and developmental offerings that UNCG provides for its students. Through its activities, the Office of Undergraduate Admissions supports the University’s enrollment goals as they pertain to new traditional and nontraditional undergraduates. Position Summary The primary purpose of this position is to function as an Admissions Counselor for for the Office of Undergraduate Admissions. This position will recruit and cultivate prospects, applicants, and admitted students through direct participation in a variety of activities, articulate admissions procedures and requirements to prospective students, parents and counselors, evaluate credentials and make independent decisions regarding individuals’ candidacies for admission to UNCG , and positively affect admitted students’ decisions to matriculate to UNCG . Minimum Qualifications A relevant Bachelor’s degree, ability to work independently with little to no supervision, strong interpersonal skills, a valid driver’s license, ability to make research based decisions regarding the potential for success of specific recruitment initiatives. Additional Required Certifications, Licensures, and Certificates Preferred Qualifications One year experience in admissions. Bilingual. Special Instructions to Applicants Recruitment Range $41,970 - $43,888 Org #-Department Undergraduate Admissions - 10202 Job Open Date 06/26/2026 For Best Consideration Date Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes FTE 1.000 Type of Appointment Permanent If time-limited, please specify end date for appointment. Number of Months per Year 12 FLSA Exempt Key Responsibilities ________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________ Percentage Of Time 40% Key Responsibility Recruitment Essential Tasks Recruiting and cultivating prospects, applicants, and admitted students, including first generation and rural areas in NC, through direct participation in a variety of activities. Visit high school and community organizations to recruit prospective students. Attend college fairs. Sponsoring recruitment events and Undergraduate Admissions programs. Sending email, writing letters, and placing calls to students. Percentage Of Time 25% Key Responsibility Counseling Essential Tasks Support Latinx students and their families throughout the application enrollment process. Represents the University in the community. Providing admitted students information on completing the enrollment process and serve as a referral to assist students on and off campus as needed. Counseling students on how to improve their academic record and increase chance of admission. Percentage Of Time 25% Key Responsibility Admissions Decisions Essential Tasks Articulate admissions procedures and requirements to prospective students, parents and counselors. Evaluate credentials and make independent decisions regarding individuals’ candidacies for admission. Positively affect admitted students’ decisions to matriculate to UNCG . Percentage Of Time 10% Key Responsibility Other duties as assigned Essential Tasks Other duties as assigned. ADA Checklist ADA Checklist R for Rare (0-30%), O for Occasional (30-60%), F for Frequent (60-90%), C for Constant (90-100%). Physical Effort Reading - F, Writing - F, Hearing - F, Talking - F, Standing - O, Sitting - F Work Environment Inside - F, Outside - O Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume/CV Cover Letter List of References Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Please describe your previous Admissions or Enrollment Management experience? (Open Ended Question) * This position requires extensive travel in/out of North Carolina. Additionally, evening and weekend hours related to recruitment programs.  Are you able to fulfill this requirement? yes no * Do you have a valid North Carolina Driver's License? Yes No * Are you Bilingual?  If yes, please indicate which languages you proficiently speak. (Open Ended Question) * Please indicate how you learned of the vacant position for which you are applying: SpartanTalent Website Piedmont Triad Area Newspaper The Chronicle of Higher Education Inside Higher Ed Other professional journal / website NCWorks.gov UNC School System Job Board Personal Networking Facebook Twitter Indeed.com NorthCarolinaDiversity.com Other * If you selected \"Other\", please provide the name of the resource here. (Open Ended Question) * Are you eligible to work in the United States without sponsorship? Yes No * Will you now or in the future require visa sponsorship for employment at the University of North Carolina in Greensboro? Yes No UNCG Human Resources UNC Greensboro 723 Kenilworth St. Greensboro, NC 27402-6170 Telephone 336.334.5009 Fax 336.334.5585 Back to top Copyright © 2025. UNC Greensboro. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://spartantalent.uncg.edu/postings/35902": {
      "title": "Director of Financial Planning and Budgets",
      "description": "UNC Greensboro Employment Opportunities | Director of Financial Planning and Budgets Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Current UNCG Employee Log In UNC System Careers Director of Financial Planning and Budgets Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Please see Special Instructions for more details. UNC Greensboro has retained the services of The UNC System Office Executive Search Firm to support this recruitment. Confidential inquiries and nominations should be directed to them. To apply, please visit https://northcarolina.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/6/home/requisition/746?c=northcarolina to submit a cover letter and CV/resume. Confidential review of applications will begin immediately and the position remains open until filled. Posting Details Position Information Position Number 000173 Functional Title Director of Financial Planning and Budgets Position Type Staff Position Eclass EP - EHRA 12 mo leave earning University Information Located in North Carolina’s third largest city, UNC Greensboro is among the most diverse, learner-centered public research universities in the state, with 18,000 students in eight colleges and schools pursuing more than 150 areas of undergraduate and over 200 areas of graduate study. UNCG continues to be recognized nationally for academic excellence, access, and affordability. UNCG is ranked No. 1 most affordable institution in North Carolina for net cost by the N.Y. Times and No. 1 in North Carolina for social mobility by The Wall Street Journal — helping first-generation and lower-income students find paths to prosperity. Designated an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University by the Association of Public and Land-grant Universities, UNCG is a community-engaged research institution with a portfolio of more than $67M in research and creative activity. The University’s 2,600 staff help create an annual economic impact for the Piedmont Triad region in excess of $1B. Primary Purpose of the Organizational Unit The Finance team within the Division of Finance and Administration (F&A) supports the University’s mission, strategic vision, goals, and objectives by providing key financial services efficiently and effectively. The Finance team provides financial services directly to students and the campus community. Units within Finance include Contract & Grant Accounting, Controller’s Office, Financial Planning & Budgets and Procurement Services. Financial Planning & Budgets is specifically responsible for providing university budgetary financial reports, projections, and analysis as well as administering all budgeting functions for the operation of UNC Greensboro. They maintain fiscal integrity through development and administration of financial and budgetary control functions. This team works with other areas within the larger F&A Division to provide accurate and complete financial budget data to university management and state and federal agencies. A strong commitment to customer service, collaboration, and team development is central to the division’s mission. Position Summary The Director of Financial Planning and Budgets provides strategic leadership for the university’s comprehensive budget development, financial planning, and analytical functions. This role oversees the direction, administration, and continuous improvement of complex budget processes, ensuring alignment with institutional priorities and full compliance with university, state, and federal requirements. Serving as a key advisor to senior leadership, the Director leads a team responsible for developing reports, interpreting financial data, modeling scenarios, projecting revenues and expenditures, and identifying trends that inform strategic decision‑making. The position maintains direct communication with the Office of State Budget and Management ( OSBM ), the UNC System Office ( UNCSO ), and campus leaders including Vice Chancellors, Deans, Department Heads, and Directors. The Director establishes and maintains effective budget procedures, prepares high‑level analyses and presentations for executive leadership, evaluates financial options, and reports on outcomes of budgetary decisions. Core functional responsibilities include team building, customer service, managerial oversight, reporting, planning and projections, reversion planning, coordination of carryforwards and special allocations, debt‑related issues, campus training, all other general budget matters, and addressing special requests. Professionalism, collaboration, and sound judgment are essential to success in this role. Minimum Qualifications Masters and 5+ years of relevant experience; or Bachelors with at least 1-2 yrs supervisory experience and 5+ years of relevant experience. Additional Required Certifications, Licensures, and Certificates Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree in business administration, public administration, or a related field is preferred, with prior supervisory experience in a complex operational area. Demonstrated strong proficiency with ERP systems, preferably Banner by Ellucian, along with excellent analytical skills, attention to detail, and the ability to communicate effectively in both written and verbal formats with leadership, colleagues across campus and off-campus entities at the state level. University or community college Budget Office and financial administration experience within North Carolina’s public higher education environment, with eight years of public‑sector financial administration is desired, including at least three years within the North Carolina community college or university system. Commitment to inclusive leadership, the ability to set and achieve goals, and the capacity to work collaboratively with a cross-functional team are essential. Special Instructions to Applicants UNC Greensboro has retained the services of The UNC System Office Executive Search Firm to support this recruitment. Confidential inquiries and nominations should be directed to them. To apply, please visit https://northcarolina.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/6/home/requisition/746?c=northcarolina to submit a cover letter and CV/resume. Confidential review of applications will begin immediately and the position remains open until filled. Recruitment Range $150,000 - $165,000 Org #-Department Fin Planning and Budgets - 56203 Job Open Date 08/06/2026 For Best Consideration Date Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes FTE 1.000 Type of Appointment Permanent If time-limited, please specify end date for appointment. Number of Months per Year 12 FLSA Exempt Key Responsibilities ________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________ Percentage Of Time 20% Key Responsibility Enterprise Financial Strategy Essential Tasks Provide strategic leadership in partnership with the VC for Finance and Administration and the AVC for Finance for the University’s enterprise financial planning efforts by aligning financial resources with institutional priorities, the University’s Strategic Plan, and long-term fiscal sustainability. Strategic Planning: Lead development of the University’s annual, multi-year, and long-range financial plans. Align institutional financial resources with strategic priorities. Evaluate the financial impact of strategic initiatives, new academic programs, enrollment strategies, research initiatives, and capital investments. Develop financial sustainability strategies and recommend long-term funding solutions. Identify financial risks and opportunities and recommend mitigation strategies. Lead the integration of financial planning with enrollment management, human resources, facilities, research administration, information technology, and institutional strategic planning to support enterprise-wide decision making and resource alignment. Collaborate with executive leadership to ensure financial strategies support institutional goals and operational priorities. Institutional Policy: Develop and recommend University budget policies and financial planning guidelines. Coordinate development of biennial legislative budget requests and strategic funding proposals. Participate in institutional strategic planning initiatives. Advise executive leadership on financial implications of institutional initiatives. Percentage Of Time 15% Key Responsibility Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) Essential Tasks Lead the University’s comprehensive financial planning, forecasting, budgeting, and analytical functions while ensuring accurate, timely, and compliant budget administration. Budget Development: Direct development and administration of the University’s annual operating budget for all university funds. Develop All Funds Budget Coordinate the annual budget planning calendar and budget development process. Develop budget instructions, assumptions, and allocation methodologies. Review and recommend departmental budget requests. Prepare consolidated University budget recommendations. Budget Administration: Monitor budgets throughout the fiscal year. Review budget revisions and transfers. Ensure budget transactions comply with University, UNC System, and State requirements. Maintain budget controls and internal financial controls. Coordinate year-end budget closeout activities. Oversee position budgeting and salary reserve planning. Monitor vacancy savings and salary projections. Financial Planning & Analytics: Develop multi-year financial forecasts. Prepare enrollment, tuition, personnel, benefits, and State appropriation forecasts. Manage the tuition and fee process on campus Conduct financial modeling and scenario analyses. Develop predictive and prescriptive financial analytics that identify emerging trends, opportunities, operational efficiencies, and potential financial challenges before they materialize. Monitor institutional financial performance against budget and forecast. Develop executive dashboards and key financial indicators. Evaluate financial trends and recommend corrective actions. Maintain enterprise financial planning systems, including Anaplan and related technologies. Reporting: Prepare recurring financial reports for executive leadership. Develop budget reports for the UNC System Office and State agencies. Produce analyses supporting accreditation, audits, and financial reporting. Ensure accuracy and integrity of institutional budget data. Percentage Of Time 20% Key Responsibility Strategic Resource Allocation Essential Tasks Lead strategic allocation of University resources to maximize institutional effectiveness while ensuring fiscal accountability and responsible stewardship of public funds. Develop funding allocation models aligned with institutional priorities. Evaluate recurring and one-time funding requests. Coordinate allocation of enrollment growth funding, tuition revenue, State appropriations, and institutional reserves. Develop reserve, carryforward, and contingency funding strategies. Support capital planning and debt affordability analyses. Analyze financial implications of organizational restructuring. Recommend budget reallocations based on institutional priorities. Evaluate return on investment for strategic initiatives. Monitor resource utilization and recommend operational efficiencies. Develop institutional financial performance measures and key performance indicators (KPIs) that evaluate the effectiveness of resource allocation decisions and assess progress toward strategic goals. Regularly assess organizational financial performance against established metrics and recommend resource adjustments to improve institutional outcomes. Percentage Of Time 15% Key Responsibility Executive Decision Support Essential Tasks Serve as the principal financial planning advisor to the Associate Vice Chancellor for Finance by providing executive-level financial analyses, strategic recommendations, and budget expertise. Prepare executive financial reports for the Chancellor, Cabinet, Board of Trustees, and executive leadership. Develop presentations and briefing materials. Conduct financial analyses supporting strategic initiatives. Evaluate legislative, UNC System, and regulatory changes affecting University finances. Advise Deans, Vice Chancellors, and senior administrators on budget development and financial planning. Represent the University with the UNC System Office, Office of State Budget and Management, Office of State Controller, and other State agencies. Present budget recommendations to executive leadership. Respond to financial information requests from internal and external stakeholders. Conduct enterprise financial risk assessments, scenario planning, and stress testing to evaluate the potential impact of changing economic conditions, enrollment fluctuations, legislative actions, and other strategic risks on the University’s financial position. Provide executive leadership with strategic recommendations to enhance financial resilience and support informed decision-making under varying financial scenarios. Develop and maintain new program financial/budget planning templates and advise Dean and Program Managers on budget development for their proposed or modified programs Percentage Of Time 10% Key Responsibility Business Transformation and Innovation Essential Tasks Lead modernization of the University’s financial planning and budgeting functions through technology, process improvement, data analytics, and organizational innovation. Lead continuous improvement of budget development processes. Implement and optimize enterprise planning systems, including Anaplan. Improve integration between Banner, budgeting, and reporting systems. Develop automated reporting and dashboard capabilities. Promote the use of predictive analytics, business intelligence, automation, and artificial intelligence to improve financial forecasting and decision-making. Simplify and standardize budget procedures. Develop financial data governance standards. Benchmark budgeting and financial planning practices against peer institutions. Lead departmental process improvement initiatives. Percentage Of Time 10% Key Responsibility Organizational Leadership Essential Tasks Provide strategic and operational leadership for the Office of Financial Planning and Budgets while fostering a collaborative, customer-focused, and high-performing organization. Leadership: Direct daily operations of the Office of Financial Planning and Budgets. Recruit, supervise, mentor, and evaluate professional staff. Establish departmental priorities, goals, and performance expectations. Develop succession and professional development plans. Collaboration: Build strong partnerships with colleges, divisions, and administrative units. Provide financial consultation and high level of customer service across campus. Promote transparency and collaborative budget planning. Represent the department on University committees and strategic initiatives. Collaborate with the Controller’s Office to lead the University’s fiscal year-end financial planning and closeout activities by monitoring budget performance, evaluating projected year-end financial results, recommending strategic use of available resources, managing carryforward and reserve targets, and ensuring an accurate, compliant, and timely fiscal year-end close. Compliance: Ensure compliance with University, UNC System, State, and federal policies. Coordinate responses to financial audits. Develop and maintain departmental operating procedures. Ensure effective internal controls and stewardship of university resources. ADA Checklist ADA Checklist R for Rare (0-30%), O for Occasional (30-60%), F for Frequent (60-90%), C for Constant (90-100%). Physical Effort Finger Dexterity - F, Reading - F, Writing - F, Vision-Preparing/Analyzing figures - F, Hearing - F, Talking - F, Standing - O, Sitting - F, Hand Movement-Repetitive Motions, Hand Movement-Grasping, Holding, Eye-hand coordination, Vision-Color Distinction Work Environment Inside - F Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume/CV Cover Letter List of References Reference Letter 1 Reference Letter 2 Reference Letter 3 Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have experience with Banner Finance and/or Banner Student? Yes No * If you have previous experience working with State Institution/Agency, please explain. (Open Ended Question) * Are you currently a permanent, time-limited or RIF employee of UNCG? No Response Yes No * Please indicate how you learned of the vacant position for which you are applying: UNCGjobsearch Website Greensboro News & Record Carolina Peacemaker Other Newspaper The Chronicle of Higher Education Inside Higher Ed Other Professional Journal NCWorks.gov UNC System Job Board Other Online Job Board Personal Networking Other * If you selected \"Other\", please provide the name of the resource here. (Open Ended Question) UNCG Human Resources UNC Greensboro 723 Kenilworth St. Greensboro, NC 27402-6170 Telephone 336.334.5009 Fax 336.334.5585 Back to top Copyright © 2025. UNC Greensboro. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://spartantalent.uncg.edu/postings/35560": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Fitness Operations",
      "description": "UNC Greensboro Employment Opportunities | Coordinator, Fitness Operations Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Current UNCG Employee Log In UNC System Careers Coordinator, Fitness Operations Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Position Number 997940 Functional Title Coordinator, Fitness Operations Position Type Staff Position Eclass EP - EHRA 12 mo leave earning University Information Located in North Carolina’s third largest city, UNC Greensboro is among the most diverse, learner-centered public research universities in the state, with 18,000 students in eight colleges and schools pursuing more than 150 areas of undergraduate and over 200 areas of graduate study. UNCG continues to be recognized nationally for academic excellence, access, and affordability. UNCG is ranked No. 1 most affordable institution in North Carolina for net cost by the N.Y. Times and No. 1 in North Carolina for social mobility by The Wall Street Journal — helping first-generation and lower-income students find paths to prosperity. Designated an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University by the Association of Public and Land-grant Universities, UNCG is a community-engaged research institution with a portfolio of more than $67M in research and creative activity. The University’s 2,600 staff help create an annual economic impact for the Piedmont Triad region in excess of $1B. Primary Purpose of the Organizational Unit The Department of Recreation and Wellness (RecWell) is housed in the Division of Student Affairs. RecWell fosters comprehensive wellness by engaging students and the UNCG community in experiential and educational opportunities. We promote lifelong success through meaningful and inclusive programs and resources. RecWell offers comprehensive recreation and wellness programming including; fitness, aquatics, informal recreation, outdoor adventures, competitive sports, Esports, and wellness education. The department strives to provide opportunities that enhance student success. The Division of Student Affairs encourages students to be engaged citizens through enriching their personal and professional development in a diverse and inclusive community of care. Student Affairs goals include: Collaborating to create a vibrant student-centered environment supports student learning, engagement, service, and leadership while preparing students to contribute to a global society. Create a culture of care and support for all students which empowers them to take an active and influential role in their collegiate experience. Cultivate opportunities to enrich the student experience and the University’s rich traditions in celebration of diversity, service, life-long learning and Spartan Pride. Serve as wise stewards of University resources. Position Summary Under the direction and supervision of the Assistant Director of Fitness, the Coordinator, Fitness Operations is responsible for the coordination and management of the fitness spaces in the 216,000 sq ft. Leonard J. Kaplan Center for Wellness. The Coordinator, Fitness Operations also directly supervises and develops a team of approximately 30–40 part-time student employees through recruitment, hiring, onboarding, scheduling, training, evaluating, and ongoing professional development. The position is responsible for maintaining service excellence by ensuring staff are knowledgeable in customer service, equipment usage, emergency response procedures, and risk management practices. Minimum Qualifications Relevant Bachelor’s degree with relevant experience. National certification in group fitness and/or personal training. Team-player philosophy and able to successfully work independently and in collaborative groups. Strong communication skills, both oral and written. Strong commitment to the development of students to provide leadership training, support academic success, and professional development. American Red Cross CPR / AED and First Aid Certification or able to attain within first three months. Ability to work occasional evening and weekend hours in addition to regular University hours Additional Required Certifications, Licensures, and Certificates American Red Cross CPR / AED and First Aid certification or able to attain within first three months. Drivers license required. National certification in group exercise and/or personal training. Preferred Qualifications Master’s Degree in Sport Management, Exercise Science/Kinesiology, or related field from a 4-year accredited institution. Two years’ professional experience in recreation, athletics or fitness in higher education or commercial setting (completion of a Graduate Assistantship will be counted in professional experience). Experience managing a fitness facility with oversight of equipment preventative maintenance and assessment, budgetary and/or equipment purchasing. Marketing, promotions, communications and event-hosting experience. Competent in recreation/fitness software including, but not limited to: Fusion,Connect2 and Time Clock Plus, When2Work, Canvas, EMS , Handshake. Special Instructions to Applicants Recruitment Range $43,888 - $46,000 Org #-Department Recreation and Wellness - 44801 Job Open Date 07/30/2026 For Best Consideration Date Job Close Date Open Until Filled No FTE 1.000 Type of Appointment Permanent If time-limited, please specify end date for appointment. Number of Months per Year 12 FLSA Exempt Key Responsibilities ________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________ Percentage Of Time 40 Key Responsibility Area Leadership Essential Tasks Coordinate and manage the daily operations of the fitness spaces of the Leonard J. Kaplan Center for Wellness to reflect industry standards and trends. Maintain the quality (cleanliness/appearance) of fitness spaces and equipment Assist in the development and implementation of the fitness program area’s long-term and short-term goals. Manage program area budgets for fitness operations and fitness events. Assist with equipment replacement planning and capital purchases. Evaluates and recommends fitness equipment for purchase, repair parts, materials and supplies required to meet program and facility needs. Percentage Of Time 30 Key Responsibility Supervision Essential Tasks Recruit, hire, train, supervise and evaluate staff including approximately 30-40 student staff. Shared supervision and evaluation of the Graduate Assistant. Evaluate, assess and revise staff training, manuals and in-service trainings to meet industry standards and department mission and values. Develop learning outcomes and assessments for student employees. Percentage Of Time 20 Key Responsibility Programming Essential Tasks Create, expand and implement fitness and wellness programming through innovative ideas and partnerships. Assist with the implementation and execution of fitness events. Assess and evaluate the potential risks associated with fitness programs and facilities. Develop and implement policies, procedures, and guidelines for fitness spaces. Assist with teaching fitness prep course to UNCG students and university affiliates. Develop and assess program goals to align with strategic initiatives within Recreation and Wellness, Student Affairs, and UNCG Assist marketing program in promoting and marketing fitness initiatives. Instruct fitness offerings as necessary. Percentage Of Time 10 Key Responsibility Other Essential Tasks Assist with the instruction of CPRO /First Aid classes for student employees. Provide leadership for assigned departmental programs and events. Uphold and execute the department and university mission and values. Serve on department/university committees and work teams. Collaborate and communicate with direct supervisors and colleagues Flexible schedule to work some evenings, weekends and university breaks based on program offerings and schedule. Perform other related duties as assigned. ADA Checklist ADA Checklist R for Rare (0-30%), O for Occasional (30-60%), F for Frequent (60-90%), C for Constant (90-100%). Physical Effort Reading - c, Writing - c, Hearing - c, Talking - c, Standing - c, Walking - c, Lifting-0-30 lbs. - o, Lifting-30-60 lbs. - o, Pushing/Pulling - o, Bending - o, Squatting - o Work Environment Inside - c, Outside - o Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume/CV Cover Letter List of References Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Please indicate how you learned of the vacant position for which you are applying: SpartanTalent Website Piedmont Triad Area Newspaper The Chronicle of Higher Education Inside Higher Ed Other professional journal / website NCWorks.gov UNC School System Job Board Personal Networking Facebook Twitter Indeed.com NorthCarolinaDiversity.com Other * If you selected \"Other\", please provide the name of the resource here. (Open Ended Question) * Are you eligible to work in the United States without sponsorship? Yes No UNCG Human Resources UNC Greensboro 723 Kenilworth St. Greensboro, NC 27402-6170 Telephone 336.334.5009 Fax 336.334.5585 Back to top Copyright © 2025. UNC Greensboro. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://spartantalent.uncg.edu/postings/32982": {
      "title": "Collaboration Coordinator",
      "description": "UNC Greensboro Employment Opportunities | Collaboration Coordinator - West Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Current UNCG Employee Log In UNC System Careers Collaboration Coordinator - West Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Position Number 998036 Functional Title Collaboration Coordinator - West Position Type Research Position Eclass EP - EHRA 12 mo leave earning University Information Located in North Carolina’s third largest city, UNC Greensboro is among the most diverse, learner-centered public research universities in the state, with 18,000 students in eight colleges and schools pursuing more than 150 areas of undergraduate and over 200 areas of graduate study. UNCG continues to be recognized nationally for academic excellence, access, and affordability. UNCG is ranked No. 1 most affordable institution in North Carolina for net cost by the N.Y. Times and No. 1 in North Carolina for social mobility by The Wall Street Journal — helping first-generation and lower-income students find paths to prosperity. Designated an Innovation and Economic Prosperity University by the Association of Public and Land-grant Universities, UNCG is a community-engaged research institution with a portfolio of more than $67M in research and creative activity. The University’s 2,600 staff help create an annual economic impact for the Piedmont Triad region in excess of $1B. Primary Purpose of the Organizational Unit The Center for Youth, Family, and Community Partnerships ( CYFCP ) builds the capacity of families, service providers, researchers, teachers, and communities to promote the well-being of children and youth. In partnership with colleagues from across the university and the community, the center carries out basic, applied, and action research; translates research into effective programs and practice; infuses community perspectives into university research and teaching; facilitates strategic problem-solving processes; and promotes programs, practices, and policies that yield positive outcomes for children and their families. Position Summary The Collaboration Coordinator West will foster collaboration and advocate for Family Voice & Choice in partnership with other service delivery systems, State agencies, and other relevant stakeholders, including the Department of Public Instruction and SAMHSA -sponsored national agencies with a focus on the western region of NC. The Collaboration Coordinator also will work to establish a formal network or directory of different family, youth, and/or young-adult led organizations or roles within organizations across the State. This network or directory will help to facilitate future additional collaboration and coordination of individuals working to elevate the voice and perspectives of families, youths, and young adults across the state and help to make it easier than it is currently to identify who is engaged in this work throughout North Carolina. The dedicated Coordination and Collaboration role within the Family and Youth Support and Education Program will be critical to the program’s ability to support local and State Collaboratives, while also remaining true to the SOC Values and Principles. Minimum Qualifications Relevant post-Baccalaureate degree required; for candidates demonstrating comparable independent research productivity or comparable background in independent academic or instructional activities, will accept a relevant undergraduate degree and 3 or more years of relevant experience in substitution. This position requires that the employee has relevant lived experiences related to personal and/or caregiving/family experience with a Serious Emotional Disturbance for at least 12 months. This position requires that employees have experience working in some form of paid or unpaid Youth/Family Peer Specialist, Navigator, Partner, or other similar roles. Applicants should be able to demonstrate experience with skills in the following areas: written and verbal communication skills, organization, and cross-agency collaboration and coordination. Additional Required Certifications, Licensures, and Certificates Preferred Qualifications Applicants who are certified or eligible to be certified as a Certified Family Peer Specialist is preferred. Applicants who have experience working in a leadership role related to Youth and/or Family Peer Support and/or Education and applicants living in the western part of NC is preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants Recruitment Range Commensurate with Experience Org #-Department Center-Youth-Families & Comm - 11504 Job Open Date 10/03/2025 For Best Consideration Date Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes FTE 1.000 Type of Appointment Permanent If time-limited, please specify end date for appointment. Number of Months per Year 12 FLSA Exempt Key Responsibilities ________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________ Percentage Of Time 50% Key Responsibility Collaboration and Coordination Essential Tasks Partners with local and State SOC Collaboratives in the western portion of North Carolina. Promotes and advocates for Family Voice and Choice at all levels of services, systems, and policies in North Carolina. Attends and participates in local, state, and/or national meetings and conferences to provide information and gather feedback on service and system planning. Works with various State agencies and other agencies to promote SOC values and practices. Establishes and maintains relationships with SAMHSA -sponsored national agencies who have similar missions at the national level. Percentage Of Time 20% Key Responsibility Coordinating a network or directory of family, youth and/or young adult-led organizations Essential Tasks Establishes and maintains some form of formal network or directory of different family, youth, and/or young adult-led organizations or roles across the State of North Carolina. Develops plans for leveraging this network to help facilitate future collaborations and coordination activities among individuals and organizations working to elevate the voice and perspectives of families, youths, and young adults across North Carolina. Percentage Of Time 10% Key Responsibility Build Family Capacity Essential Tasks Identify and link families to partners across the State to address identified needs. Develop peer support networks across the state. Develop leadership and advocacy skills among families. Link families with state networks representing adult and youth mental health consumers. Percentage Of Time 10% Key Responsibility Program Outreach and Information Dissemination Essential Tasks Develop and update content monthly and disseminate information via program website, electronic newsletters, integrating information from ongoing technical assistance and collaboration with key federal, state, and local partners (e.g., SAMHSA , NC DMH /DD/ SAS , System of Care Collaborative, etc.). Provide technical assistance related to inquiries through the 1-800 program number. Percentage Of Time 10% Key Responsibility Statewide needs assessment to inform program goals and evaluation Essential Tasks Collaborate with the PI develop the content of the statewide needs assessment. Conduct stakeholder interviews and focus groups. Collaborate with the evaluator to translate quantitative and qualitative data into goals and objectives for the scope of work as well as develop the ongoing program evaluation. ADA Checklist ADA Checklist R for Rare (0-30%), O for Occasional (30-60%), F for Frequent (60-90%), C for Constant (90-100%). Physical Effort Hand Movement-Repetitive Motions, Reading, Writing, Hearing, Talking, Standing, Lifting-0-30 lbs. Work Environment Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume/CV Cover Letter List of References Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Please indicate how you learned of the vacant position for which you are applying: UNCGjobsearch Website Greensboro News & Record Carolina Peacemaker Other Newspaper The Chronicle of Higher Education Inside Higher Ed Other Professional Journal NCWorks.gov UNC System Job Board Other Online Job Board Personal Networking Other * Other: Please list (Open Ended Question) * Are you eligible to work in the United States without sponsorship? Yes No * Will you now or in the future require visa sponsorship for employment at the University of North Carolina in Greensboro? Yes No UNCG Human Resources UNC Greensboro 723 Kenilworth St. Greensboro, NC 27402-6170 Telephone 336.334.5009 Fax 336.334.5585 Back to top Copyright © 2025. UNC Greensboro. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
